  "content": {
    "id": "p45-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Solve $\\binom{8}{n+1}=\\binom{9}{4}-\\binom{8}{n}$ for $n$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n=3$ or $n=4$.",
      "worked": "Both coefficients require an integer $n$ with $0\\le n\\le7$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\binom{8}{n}+\\binom{8}{n+1}&=\\binom{9}{4}\\\\\\binom{8}{n}+\\binom{8}{n+1}&=126\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $n=0,1,2,3$, the sums are $9,36,84,126$, respectively. By $\\binom{8}{r}=\\binom{8}{8-r}$, the sums for $n=4,5,6,7$ are $126,84,36,9$, respectively. Hence $n=3$ or $n=4$.\n\nCheck: for $n=3$, $70=126-56$; for $n=4$, $56=126-70$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 46
  },
